1. Project Title

Sales Performance Dashboard and Business Insights using Power BI

2. Project Overview

This project focuses on analyzing sales transactions, customer information, and sales target data using Microsoft Power BI. The objective is to transform raw business data into meaningful insights through data cleaning, modeling, DAX calculations, and interactive dashboards.

The dashboard enables business stakeholders to monitor sales performance, compare actual sales against targets, evaluate Sales POCs and Sales Managers, understand customer behavior, identify regional trends, and support data-driven decision-making.

3. Problem Statement

The company receives orders from customers across 14 countries through multiple sales channels, including App, Website, WhatsApp, and Other. Due to a system bug at the beginning of the financial year, some Order Source values were not captured correctly.

Management requires an analytical dashboard to:

Identify and fix missing data.
Track sales performance against targets.
Monitor Sales Managers and Sales Teams.
Analyze customer behavior.
Study country-wise sales performance.
Understand order trends.
Improve future business decisions.
4. Objectives

The primary objectives of this project are:

Import and clean multiple datasets.
Handle missing Order Source values.
Create an optimized data model.
Develop DAX measures for business KPIs.
Compare actual sales with targets.
Analyze customer demographics.
Evaluate Sales Manager performance.
Study country-wise order trends.
Build an interactive Power BI dashboard.
Enable drill-through, bookmarks, and Q&A functionality.
5. Dataset Description

The project uses three datasets.

Orders

Contains transactional data.

Columns include:

Order ID
Customer ID
Order DateTime
Order Source
Sales POC
Order Value

Each Order ID is unique.

Customers

Contains customer information.

Columns include:

Customer ID
Gender
Age
Country
Category

Each Customer ID is unique.

Sales Targets

Contains sales hierarchy information.

Columns include:

Sales POC
Sales Manager
Sales Team
Sales Target

This table is used to compare target versus actual sales.

6. Tools Used
Microsoft Power BI Desktop
Power Query
DAX (Data Analysis Expressions)
Microsoft Excel
GitHub
7. Data Cleaning Process

The following transformations were performed using Power Query.

Orders Table
Imported Excel data.
Corrected data types.
Checked duplicate records.
Identified missing Order Source values.
Determined the first and last timestamps affected by the system bug.
Replaced missing Order Source values using an appropriate imputation technique.
Verified Order ID uniqueness.
Customers Table
Corrected data types.
Removed duplicate Customer IDs.
Verified customer information.
Sales Targets Table
Corrected data types.
Merged Sales Manager First Name and Last Name into a single Sales Manager column.
Verified Sales POC mapping.
8. Handling Missing Values

A system bug caused missing values in the Order Source column.

Approach Used

The missing categorical values were imputed using the most appropriate method based on the observed data pattern:

Fill Down (for continuous missing records)
Mode (Most Frequent Value)
Why This Approach?

Order Source is a categorical variable. Numerical methods such as mean or median are not suitable. Using the mode or Fill Down preserves the original distribution of the data and minimizes bias.

9. Data Model

Relationships created in Power BI:

Table	Relationship
Customers → Orders	Customer ID
Sales Targets → Orders	Sales POC

Relationship Type

One-to-Many

Cross Filter

Single Direction

This star-schema model improves performance and simplifies report development.

10. DAX Measures

The following measures were created.

Total Sales
Total Sales =
SUM(Orders[Order Value])
Total Orders
Total Orders =
COUNT(Orders[Order ID])
Total Customers
Total Customers =
DISTINCTCOUNT(Orders[Customer ID])
Average Order Value
Average Order Value =
AVERAGE(Orders[Order Value])
Sales Target
Sales Target =
SUM('Sales Targets'[Target])
Achievement %
Achievement % =
DIVIDE([Total Sales],[Sales Target])
Sales Gap
Sales Gap =
[Sales Target]-[Total Sales]
Target Status
Target Status =
SWITCH(
TRUE(),
[Total Sales] < [Sales Target], "Target Not Met",
[Total Sales] = [Sales Target], "Target Met",
"Exceeded Target"
)
